  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nA deep 500mb trough across the eastern U.S. will lead to several \nchilly days ahead for us. 850mb temps drop to around -5C which will \nset the stage for our first hard freeze each of the next several \nnights. Today will feature continued breezy conditions as the \npressure gradient remains tight before surface high pressure builds \nsouth out of Canada and becomes centered across the Midwest over the \nweekend. We may also have some additional isolated showers in our \nnortheast this afternoon thanks to an additional lobe of vorticity \nthat rotates through the broad trough in place.\n\nDry conditions are expected this weekend through at least mid next \nweek. A cutoff upper low forms over the Southern Plains on Sunday \nand slides across the lower Mississippi Valley on Monday. Guidance \ncontinues to be in good agreement keeping the precipitation shield \nwith this system south of our cwa. As this system pushes further \neast, upper ridging builds into the Ohio Valley Tuesday into \nWednesday. This results in temperatures pushing back toward normal \nlevels and even above as we head into the latter part of next week. \nSome solutions point to our next rain chance arriving later next \nweek while others keep us dry and warm through the end of the week \nbefore potentially bringing an unsettled period sometime around Nov \n22-23.",
